<Base
  title="Why the browser plans the route and the object decides | Proof of Concept Bakery"
  description="Why Proof of Concept Bakery plans deliveries twice, once with a fast heuristic on the server and once with VROOM in the visitor's browser, and why neither plan is trusted until the Dispatch object has checked it."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>Why the browser plans the route and the object decides</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      On a Saturday the bakery plans 63 deliveries on up to four vans twice, once on the server and
      once in your browser, and publishes neither until one object has checked the plan against its
      own numbers. This is why the work is split that way, how to read the gap between the two
      plans, and what the plan does not promise.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      The seeded Saturday has 63 stops and four vans. Dealing the stops out in the order the orders
      arrived sends each van across the city and back more often than it needs to. A good plan
      groups stops that are near each other and orders them so the van does not backtrack, and
      finding one is real computation: the number of ways to split 63 stops among four vans and
      order each van’s stops is far past what any program tries one by one.
    </p>

    <h2>What the lab shows, and what it cannot</h2>
    <p>
      The Route Lab, as read on {READ}, is a made-up bakery in Seattle with 20 to 63 orders. It
      plans the same orders two ways, as they came in and as VROOM plans them in a Web Worker, and
      reports the difference: on its busy Saturday, 3 h 12 min of driving against 5 h 34 min. Its
      page says VROOM “promises a good plan fast, and not always the best one”, and that its travel
      times assume no traffic.
    </p>
    <p>
      It compares a plan with no thought against one with a lot. It has no second planner of
      comparable quality, so it can show no gap between two good plans. It has no record of
      deliveries, so it cannot show what happens when a plan is wrong, or a stop is missing, or a
      van has already left. Its data are Seattle’s, from OSRM; the bakery’s are Salt Lake City’s,
      from openrouteservice. The bakery adds the server’s planner and the object that judges.
    </p>

    <h2>Two planners, neither exact</h2>
    <p>
      The server plans with the savings heuristic (Operations Research, 1964). Every stop starts as
      its own round trip from the bakery. Joining the end of one trip to the start of another saves{' '}
      <code>d(i,0) + d(0,j) − d(i,j)</code> minutes, where <code>d</code> is the drive time and 0 is
      the bakery. The heuristic scores every ordered pair, 63 × 62 = 3,906 of them for the Saturday,
      sorts by saving, and merges trips from the largest saving down while a van has room. It uses
      no clock and no random draw, so the same input gives the same plan. It is in{' '}
      <code>crates/domain/src/dispatch/savings.rs</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      VROOM, run in the browser as WebAssembly, builds a plan and then moves and swaps stops between
      routes until no change shortens the driving. It looks harder, so it should find a shorter plan
      more often than not. The page asks it to look at level 5 up to 40 stops and level 3 above
      that, and to stop after 5 seconds (<code>apps/web/src/lib/dispatch.ts</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Neither is exact, and the gap on the dispatch screen has to be read with that in mind. The
      screen reports the server’s total less the browser’s, as a percentage of the browser’s. That
      is a gap between two heuristics. A gap of zero says they agree, not that either is best. A
      positive gap is a floor on what the server’s plan leaves unused, since the browser’s plan is a
      real plan and the best one can only be shorter still. A negative gap, where the server’s plan
      is shorter, is possible, and the screen says so in words. The gap does not say how far VROOM
      is from the best plan, which neither planner computes. ADR 0012 sets the bakery’s own line at
      25 percent of total drive time on the seeded days, a revisit trigger, and the roadmap records
      no measured gap.
    </p>

    <h2>Neither is trusted: the object decides</h2>
    <p>
      The browser can send anything, so the browser’s plan is a request. The Dispatch object, one
      per sandbox and business day, checks it before it is stored and again before it is published,
      since stops come and go in between. Its rules have names a visitor can see in a refusal:{' '}
      <code>vans</code>, <code>stop-unknown</code>, <code>stop-twice</code>,{' '}
      <code>over-capacity</code>, <code>route-ends</code>, <code>stop-missing</code> and, since
      2026-11-08, <code>window</code>. Every stop appears once, no van carries more than its
      capacity, every route starts and ends at the bakery, no stop is left out, and no van reaches a
      stop after its delivery window closes. A plan that sends its own total is refused with 400,
      since the object does not read it. The minutes are always worked out from the object’s own
      travel-time matrix, the same arithmetic for the server’s plan and the browser’s.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is what makes the comparison fair. The page builds VROOM’s input from the same matrix,
      with every leg rounded up to whole minutes, so VROOM minimizes the figure the object will
      count. Since 2026-11-08 it also gives VROOM the times the object checks: the vans leave at
      9:00 am, a stop may be served until two hours after its window opens, and each door takes 3
      minutes. Until then the browser’s plan ignored time. A published plan is a{' '}
      <code>RoutePublished</code> event, and it is the only one that reaches the kitchen board and
      the orders. Once a van has left, the object asks the orders about it and refuses a new publish
      with 409 <code>published</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the computation is free and where it is paid</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The browser’s search is free to the server.</strong> VROOM’s files are 919,927 B of
        WebAssembly, 343,650 B gzipped (ADR 0012, as of 2026-11-04), fetched at build, checked
        against a lock file and loaded on the first solve. The visitor pays the bytes once and the
        time to solve: 548 ms for the 63-stop day in one headless Chrome run on 2026-11-05 (the
        roadmap’s PR 9 note, not a committed test).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The server’s plan is cheap by design.</strong> ADR 0012 estimated a few milliseconds
        of CPU for tens of stops. That has not been timed on its own. The Worker’s configured limit,{' '}
        <code>limits.cpu_ms</code> in <code>workers/api/wrangler.jsonc</code>, is 300 ms, where
        Cloudflare’s paid default on {READ} was 30 seconds. The bakery set it low on purpose, as a
        bill guard. A VROOM search that took 548 ms in a browser on another machine is a figure to
        compare against 300 ms, not a test that it would not fit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The travel times are paid once, offline.</strong> No routing service is called while
        a visitor plans. A build ran openrouteservice 9.10.0 on the bakery’s own machine over an
        OpenStreetMap extract with a replication time of 2026-11-05T00:58:30Z, and wrote a 64-by-64
        matrix of 64 public places into <code>tools/route-data/out/</code>. The committed files are
        the record. The roadmap says the build reran byte for byte on one machine and does not
        promise equal times on another machine or version.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A plan, not a promise</h2>
    <p>
      The times are free-flow drive times with no traffic. The totals count driving only; the time
      the last van is back adds 3 minutes at every door and any wait for a window to open. The map
      draws straight lines between stops, not roads. Delivery addresses are made up and map onto the
      64 places by index, so two customers can share a drop point. A published plan says what the
      bakery would do on a road with nobody else on it.
    </p>

    <h2>What to try</h2>
    <p>
      On the <a href="/app/dispatch/">dispatch screen</a>, plan Saturday’s stops both ways, read the
      gap as above, and publish one. Then try the{' '}
      <a href={labUrl('Route Lab')} rel="noopener">
        Route Lab
      </a>{' '}
      and add or remove an order on its map and watch both of its plans re-solve. The{' '}
      <a href={howUrl('routes')}>summary on How it’s built</a> says the same in short.
    </p>

